{
    "file": "Call_Center_Training.docx",
    "title": "Call Center Training",
    "duration": "3 Days",
    "overview": [
        "Call centers and contact centers are often one of the most visible points of interaction between an organization and its customers. Every call can influence customer confidence, satisfaction, loyalty, and the organization’s reputation. Effective agents therefore require more than telephone manners: they must combine communication, listening, questioning, call control, customer-service judgment, problem solving, accurate documentation, emotional self-management, and consistent adherence to service standards.",
        "This course provides participants with the essential skills and operating practices required for professional inbound and outbound call-center work. It covers the complete call cycle, voice and language, active listening, needs identification, call control, handling inquiries, complaints and difficult callers, holds and transfers, first-contact resolution, escalation, outbound calling, documentation, productivity, quality standards, and key call-center performance measures.",
        "The program is highly practical and uses simulated calls, role plays, listening exercises, call-flow practice, difficult-customer scenarios, documentation exercises, quality reviews, and performance feedback. Participants will develop a structured approach for managing calls efficiently while maintaining accuracy, professionalism, and a positive customer experience."
    ],
    "audience": [
        "New and existing call-center and contact-center agents, customer-service representatives, help-desk employees, teleservice personnel, telephone sales-support staff, reception and switchboard personnel, service coordinators, and employees whose roles involve frequent inbound or outbound customer calls. The course is also suitable for junior team leaders who require a practical understanding of frontline call-center standards."
    ],
    "methodology": [
        "The course uses an interactive and practice-based methodology combining short presentations, simulated inbound and outbound calls, voice and tone exercises, active-listening drills, questioning practice, call-flow simulations, hold and transfer scenarios, complaint and difficult-caller role plays, case documentation exercises, quality-monitoring activities, peer feedback, trainer coaching, and personal action planning. Emphasis is placed on repeated practice and immediate feedback."
    ],
    "competencies": [
        "Professional call handling",
        "Voice and telephone communication",
        "Active listening",
        "Customer needs identification",
        "Effective questioning",
        "Call control",
        "Problem solving",
        "Complaint handling",
        "De-escalation",
        "First-contact resolution",
        "Call documentation",
        "Call-center quality and productivity"
    ],
    "objectivesIntro": [
        "By the end of the course, participants will be able to:"
    ],
    "objectives": [
        "Explain the role of the call center in the overall customer experience.",
        "Apply a professional structure to inbound and outbound customer calls.",
        "Use voice, tone, pace, language, and listening skills to communicate effectively.",
        "Ask purposeful questions to identify customer needs and clarify problems.",
        "Control calls professionally while maintaining customer rapport.",
        "Manage holds, transfers, callbacks, and escalations according to good service practice.",
        "Handle complaints, angry callers, and difficult situations calmly and constructively.",
        "Improve first-contact resolution by combining accurate diagnosis, knowledge, and ownership.",
        "Document customer interactions clearly and accurately.",
        "Understand key call-center quality, service, and productivity measures.",
        "Manage personal performance and stress in a high-volume call environment."
    ],
